@Lazy and setter injection solve different problems in Spring Boot, although people sometimes confuse them because both affect bean initialization and dependency injection.

- Setter Injection = How a dependency is injected
- @Lazy = When a dependency is created

Let's understand both in depth.

---

1. Dependency Injection Types in Spring

Spring supports three ways of injecting dependencies.

1. Constructor Injection  (Recommended)
2. Setter Injection
3. Field Injection (Not Recommended)


Example:


Controller
      |
      v
Service
      |
      v
Repository


Spring injects the Repository into Service.

---

What is Setter Injection?

Instead of passing dependency through constructor, Spring calls a setter method.

Example

java
@Repository
public class UserRepository {

    public void save() {
        System.out.println("Saving User");
    }

}


Service

java
@Service
public class UserService {

    private UserRepository repository;

    @Autowired
    public void setRepository(UserRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    public void registerUser() {
        repository.save();
    }

}


Spring internally does


UserService service = new UserService();

UserRepository repo = new UserRepository();

service.setRepository(repo);


instead of


new UserService(repo);


---

Execution Flow

Application Starts

↓

Spring creates UserRepository

↓

Spring creates UserService

↓

Calls setter()

↓

Dependency injected

↓

Bean Ready

---

Advantages

Optional dependency

java
@Service
public class NotificationService {

    private SmsService smsService;

    @Autowired(required = false)
    public void setSmsService(SmsService smsService) {
        this.smsService = smsService;
    }
}


Application starts even if


SmsService


doesn't exist.

---

Mutable dependency

You can change dependency later.

java
service.setRepository(new TestRepository());


Useful in tests.

---

Disadvantages

Dependency may remain null.

java
@Service
public class UserService {

    private UserRepository repository;

    public void register() {

        repository.save(); // NullPointerException

    }

}


if setter never called.

Constructor injection avoids this.

---

Constructor vs Setter

Constructor

java
@Service
public class UserService {

    private final UserRepository repository;

    public UserService(UserRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

}
No — in Spring, if your class has only one constructor, you do not need to put @Autowired on it. Spring will use that constructor automatically for dependency injection.

When @Autowired is needed
If the class has multiple constructors, annotate the one Spring should use with @Autowired.

If there is only one constructor, Spring can infer it without the annotation.

Example
@Service
public class UserService {
    private final UserRepository userRepository;

    public UserService(UserRepository userRepository) {
        this.userRepository = userRepository;
    }
}
This works fine without @Autowired as long as it is the only constructor.

Practical note
Many developers still add @Autowired for clarity in older codebases, but with modern Spring, single-constructor injection is the cleanest approach.

Dependency is guaranteed.

