---

When to Use Setter Injection

Use setter injection when:

- Dependency is optional.
- You want to reconfigure or replace the dependency after object creation (common in tests).
- Legacy frameworks require JavaBean-style properties.

Avoid it for mandatory dependencies.

---

When to Use @Lazy

Use @Lazy when:

- A bean is expensive to initialize.
- The bean is rarely used.
- You need to break a circular dependency (prefer redesigning dependencies first).
- You want faster application startup.

Avoid using @Lazy everywhere, as it simply shifts initialization cost to the first use and can delay error detection.

---

Setter Injection vs @Lazy

| Feature | Setter Injection | @Lazy |
|---------|------------------|----------|
| Purpose | Inject dependency via a setter method | Delay bean creation until first use |
| Controls | How the dependency is injected | When the dependency is instantiated |
| Bean creation | Usually during application startup | Deferred until accessed |
| Best for | Optional or replaceable dependencies | Expensive or infrequently used beans |
| Can help with circular dependencies | No | Yes (by injecting a proxy) |
| Risk | Dependency may remain null if not injected | First access may incur initialization delay |
| Recommended for required dependencies | No | Can still be used with constructor injection if lazy loading is desired |

Best Practice

For most Spring Boot applications:

- ✅ Prefer constructor injection for required dependencies because it makes dependencies explicit, immutable, and easy to test.
- ✅ Use setter injection only for genuinely optional or configurable dependencies.
- ✅ Use @Lazy selectively for expensive beans or to resolve unavoidable circular dependencies, not as a general performance optimization.